.\" vi: set sw=4 ts=4:
.TH MY_NET 1 "2026-05-15" "BusyBox Diagnostic Toolkit" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
my_net \- network connection monitor: socket listing, TCP state tracking, and anomaly detection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B my_net
[\fB\-t\fR]
[\fB\-u\fR]
[\fB\-a\fR]
[\fB\-l\fR]
[\fB\-n\fR]
[\fB\-p\fR]
[\fB\-b\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR \fISTATE\fR]
[\fB\-w\fR \fISEC\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B my_net
displays TCP and UDP socket information by reading the kernel's procfs
interfaces
.IR /proc/net/tcp ,
.IR /proc/net/tcp6 ,
.IR /proc/net/udp ,
and
.IR /proc/net/udp6 .
.PP
In addition to basic socket listing, it provides:
.IP \(bu 2
TCP state machine tracking \(em each connection's state is decoded from the
kernel's hex representation into a human-readable name (ESTABLISHED,
TIME_WAIT, LISTEN, \&...).
.IP \(bu 2
Connection anomaly detection \(em after each scan,
.B my_net
checks state counts against empirically derived thresholds and prints a
warning block when a potential problem is detected (SYN flood, connection
leak, excessive TIME_WAIT, etc.).
.IP \(bu 2
PID and program name resolution \(em with
.BR \-p ,
.B my_net
walks
.I /proc/<pid>/fd/
symlinks to map socket inodes to the owning process, then performs a
sorted binary search for O(log n) lookup.
.IP \(bu 2
Watch mode \(em periodic live refresh suitable for real-time monitoring on
a terminal, compatible with the
.B \-b
batch flag for script use.
.PP
The output format is broadly compatible with
.BR ss (8)
and
.BR netstat (8)
for easy drop-in comparison.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t
Show TCP sockets only.
This is the default when neither
.B \-u
nor
.B \-a
is given.
.TP
.B \-u
Show UDP sockets only.
Suppresses TCP output; the State column shows
.B \-
for all UDP entries since UDP is stateless.
.TP
.B \-a
Show all sockets (TCP and UDP).
Equivalent to combining
.B \-t
and
.BR \-u .
.TP
.B \-l
Show only listening sockets.
Filters to connections whose TCP state is
.B LISTEN
(state code 0x0A); UDP sockets are excluded.
Compatible with
.BR "ss \-l" .
.TP
.B \-n
Numeric mode.
Reserved for future hostname resolution suppression.
Currently all addresses are printed in numeric form
.RI ( addr : port )
regardless of this flag, since
.B my_net
reads directly from procfs rather than invoking
.BR getaddrinfo (3).
.TP
.B \-p
Show PID and program name.
.B my_net
scans
.I /proc/<pid>/fd/
for each running process to build an inode\(->PID map.
Non-root users can only resolve sockets owned by their own processes;
unresolvable entries display
.BR \- .
Running as root reveals the owner of every socket.
.TP
.B \-b
Batch mode.
Disables all ANSI colour sequences and TUI control codes.
Output is plain text suitable for
.BR grep (1),
.BR awk (1),
or log redirection.
.B my_net
also enters batch mode automatically when stdout is not a terminal (pipe,
file redirect, etc.).
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fISTATE\fR
Filter connections by TCP state name.
The match is case-insensitive, so
.B LISTEN
and
.B listen
are equivalent.
See the
.B TCP STATES
section for valid values.
This option may be combined with
.B \-l
(though
.B \-l
alone is faster).
.TP
\fB\-w\fR \fISEC\fR
Watch mode: clear the screen and re-run the scan every
.I SEC
seconds.
.I SEC
is clamped to a minimum of 1.
If
.I SEC
is omitted or invalid, the default interval is 2 seconds.
Press
.B Q
(or
.BR q )
to quit and restore the terminal.
Requires a terminal on stdout; combine with
.B \-b
to get periodic plain-text output suitable for a script (the screen is
not cleared in that case).
.SH OUTPUT COLUMNS
.TP
.B Proto
Protocol family:
.BR tcp ,
.BR tcp6 ,
.BR udp ,
or
.BR udp6 .
.TP
.B State
TCP state name for TCP sockets; a bare
.B \-
for UDP sockets (which are stateless at the kernel level).
.TP
.B Local\ Address
Local IP address and port number in the form
.IR addr : port .
IPv6 addresses are bracketed:
.RI [ addr ]: port .
.TP
.B Foreign\ Address
Remote IP address and port number in the same format.
For listening sockets the remote address is
.B 0.0.0.0:0
(IPv4) or
.BR [::]:0
(IPv6).
.TP
.B PID/Program
Only present when
.B \-p
is given.
Shows the owning process as
.IR pid / name ,
or
.B \-
if the socket could not be attributed to any process (permission denied,
or the socket is in a different network namespace).
.TP
.B User
Username of the socket owner, looked up from the UID field in procfs.
A lightweight 32-entry cache avoids repeated
.BR getpwuid (3)
calls when many sockets share the same owner.
.SH TCP STATES
The following TCP state names are recognised by
.B \-s
and printed in the State column.
They correspond to the values defined in RFC\ 793 and
.IR <linux/tcp.h> .
.PP
.TS
tab(|) allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
State name|Code|Meaning
ESTABLISHED|0x01|Data transfer in progress
SYN_SENT|0x02|Active open: SYN sent, awaiting SYN-ACK
SYN_RECV|0x03|Passive open: SYN received, SYN-ACK sent
FIN_WAIT1|0x04|Active close: FIN sent, awaiting FIN or ACK
FIN_WAIT2|0x05|FIN acknowledged; awaiting remote FIN
TIME_WAIT|0x06|Waiting 2×MSL before full close
CLOSE|0x07|Connection fully closed
CLOSE_WAIT|0x08|Passive close: remote FIN received; local close pending
LAST_ACK|0x09|Passive close FIN sent; awaiting final ACK
LISTEN|0x0A|Server socket accepting connections
CLOSING|0x0B|Both sides closing simultaneously
.TE
.SH ANOMALY DETECTION
After each scan
.B my_net
compares per-state counts against the following thresholds.
When any threshold is exceeded, an
.B [ANOMALY DETECTED]
block is appended after the TCP state summary.
.PP
.TS
tab(|) allbox;
lb rb lb
l r l.
State|Threshold|Likely cause
TIME_WAIT|> 500|High connection churn; consider enabling \fInet.ipv4.tcp_tw_reuse\fR
CLOSE_WAIT|> 20|Application not calling \fBclose\fR(2) \(em connection leak
SYN_RECV|> 100|Possible SYN flood attack
LAST_ACK|> 50|Peer not responding to FIN (network fault or remote crash)
FIN_WAIT2|> 100|Half-open connection backlog; check \fInet.ipv4.tcp_fin_timeout\fR
ESTABLISHED|> 10000|Unusually high connection count; possible resource leak
.TE
.PP
Thresholds are derived from common production experience and Linux kernel
documentation.
They are intentionally conservative; a single warning does not always
indicate a real problem but merits investigation.
.SH WATCH MODE
.B my_net \-w
.I SEC
runs in a loop, clearing the terminal and re-printing the full connection
table every
.I SEC
seconds.
The status bar at the top of the screen shows the current refresh interval
and which protocol families are active.
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb.
Key|Action
Q or q|Quit watch mode and restore the terminal
.TE
.PP
.B my_net
hides the cursor during watch mode and restores it on exit.
If stdout is not a terminal, watch mode is rejected with an error; use
.B \-b
for non-interactive periodic output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid argument, missing terminal (for
.B \-w
without
.BR \-b ),
or unrecoverable system call failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List all TCP connections (default):
.B my_net
.TP
List all TCP connections with PID and program name (run as root for full view):
.B my_net \-p
.TP
Show only listening TCP sockets with owner:
.B my_net \-l \-p
.TP
Show all sockets (TCP + UDP) in batch mode, suitable for scripting:
.B my_net \-a \-b
.TP
Filter to ESTABLISHED connections only:
.B my_net \-s ESTABLISHED
.TP
Show UDP sockets with PID:
.B my_net \-u \-p
.TP
Watch all connections, refreshing every 3 seconds:
.B my_net \-a \-w 3
.TP
Count ESTABLISHED connections, compare with ss(8):
.nf
my_net \-s ESTABLISHED \-b | grep \-cE \(aq^tcp\(aq
ss \-tn | grep \-c ESTAB
.fi
.TP
Log connection state every 10 seconds to a file:
.B my_net \-a \-b \-w 10 >> /var/log/net_snapshot.log
.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.SS Address Parsing
IPv4 addresses in
.I /proc/net/tcp
are stored as a single little-endian 32-bit integer in hexadecimal.
.B my_net
reads each address with a custom 8-character hex parser
.RI ( parse_hex8 )
and casts directly to
.B in_addr_t
for
.BR inet_ntoa (3).
.PP
IPv6 addresses in
.I /proc/net/tcp6
are stored as four consecutive host-endian 32-bit words.
Each word is converted via
.BR htonl (3)
before being packed into an
.B in6_addr
and formatted with
.BR inet_ntop (3).
The bracketed
.RI [ addr ]: port
notation follows RFC\ 3986.
.SS PID Resolution
When
.B \-p
is given,
.B my_net
builds an inode\(->PID map by scanning
.I /proc/<pid>/fd/
for each process using the
.BR getdents64 (2)
system call directly (avoiding
.BR opendir / readdir
overhead).
Symlinks of the form
.B socket:[<inode>]
are recorded.
The resulting table is sorted by inode and searched with binary search,
giving O(log n) lookup per socket entry.
Non-root users will only see sockets they own; other entries show
.BR \- .
.SS Memory and I/O Optimisation
To minimise heap allocation on the critical path:
.IP \(bu 2
A static 1024-entry memory pool replaces per-entry
.BR xzalloc .
The pool is reset (index = 0) between scans rather than individually freed.
.IP \(bu 2
.I /proc/net/*
files are read into a single 256 KB static buffer with one
.BR read (2)
call per file, avoiding repeated small reads.
.IP \(bu 2
A 128 KB output buffer accumulates formatted lines and is flushed in bulk,
reducing the number of
.BR write (2)
system calls.
.IP \(bu 2
A 32-entry UID\(->username cache avoids redundant
.BR getpwuid (3)
lookups when many sockets share the same owner.
.SS Batch / Non-TTY Detection
If stdout is not connected to a terminal (pipe, file redirection, etc.),
.B my_net
automatically activates batch mode and suppresses all ANSI escape sequences,
even if
.B \-b
was not specified.
This ensures clean plain-text output when used in shell pipelines.
